---
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import WirelessBridgeDiagram from '../components/WirelessBridgeDiagram.astro';
import { faqs } from '../config/faqs';

const description =
	'Point-to-point wireless bridges, including 60 GHz where the path fits, to connect garages, workshops, and other Colorado buildings without trenching.';
---

<ServiceLayout
	title="Wireless Bridge Installation"
	description={description}
	eyebrow="Wireless bridges"
	heading="Connect buildings without trenching."
	lede="A detached garage, workshop, barn, or guest house can join the main network without a trench across the yard — when the path between the buildings will actually carry a link."
	crumb="Wireless Bridges"
	serviceName="Point-to-point wireless bridge installation"
	serviceType="Wireless bridge installation"
	faqs={faqs.bridges}
	ctaLabel="Connect Your Outbuilding"
	ctaTitle="Connect your outbuilding."
	ctaText="The distance, what blocks the view, and what the far building needs to run are the whole question."
	related={[
		{ href: '/wifi-design/', label: 'Professional WiFi', text: 'Once the link lands, the far building still needs a proper access point.' },
		{ href: '/security-cameras/', label: 'Security cameras', text: 'A frequent reason to reach a second structure.' },
		{ href: '/unifi-networking/', label: 'UniFi networking', text: 'The bridge is an extension of the main network, not a second hobby router.' },
	]}
>
	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="problem-heading">Trenching is sometimes the wrong expense.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Fiber or Ethernet in conduit is excellent when the path is open and the run is justified. It is a poor
					default when it means cutting a finished drive, a garden, or a long span to a barn for a workshop that
					needs ordinary connectivity.
				</p>
				<p>
					A properly engineered point-to-point bridge extends the main network across that gap. The remote building
					then behaves like part of the property, not like a separate internet account.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="diagram-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2 id="diagram-heading">How the link is arranged.</h2>
			<p class="lede">
				60 GHz radios are used where a short, clean path can carry a great deal of traffic. Other frequencies are
				considered when the span is longer or the view is imperfect.
			</p>
			<WirelessBridgeDiagram />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="uses-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="uses-heading">Where a bridge earns its place.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<ul>
					<li>Detached garages and workshops</li>
					<li>Barns, warehouses, and equipment buildings</li>
					<li>Guest houses and remote offices</li>
					<li>Camera locations that are awkward to cable</li>
				</ul>
				<p>On the far side, a solid link can support:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>WiFi and computers</li>
					<li>Security cameras</li>
					<li>Voice and other smart devices</li>
					<li>A local switch and ordinary internet use</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="honest-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="honest-heading">Line of sight decides more than the brochure.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					A clear path is the reliable case, especially at 60 GHz. Trees, rooflines, and foliage that fills in by
					July can weaken or block a link that looked fine in March. Distance depends on the radios, the antennas,
					weather, and how much performance you need at the far end. There is no single range that is honest for
					every property.
				</p>
				<p>
					If the path is marginal, the recommendation may still be conduit. The point of the visit is to know which
					one before money is spent.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ServiceLayout>
